WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Here are two beakers of acid, hydrochloric acid

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.240
and acetic acid, the acid in vinegar.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.040
Both labels say zero point one zero moles

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.760
per litre, so each litre has the same

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.680
amount of acid dissolved.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.079
But a pH meter reads one point zero

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.800
for the hydrochloric acid, and two point nine

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.640
for the acetic acid.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.680
Drop in magnesium, and the hydrochloric acid fizzes

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.600
hard while the acetic acid barely bubbles.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.080
Same amount of acid, so why such a

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:29.840
difference?

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:31.786
Zoom in.

00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:36.026
In the hydrochloric acid, every hydrogen chloride molecule

00:00:36.107 --> 00:00:38.826
has given its proton to a water molecule,

00:00:38.907 --> 00:00:41.866
making hydronium ions and chloride ions.

00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:44.746
In the acetic acid, most molecules are still

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:45.786
whole.

00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.506
At any moment, only about one molecule in

00:00:48.587 --> 00:00:51.066
eighty has given its proton away.

00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:54.106
Hydronium ions are what make a solution acidic,

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:56.426
so the acetic acid has far fewer of

00:00:56.507 --> 00:00:56.826
them.

00:00:58.034 --> 00:01:00.113
That difference is called strength.

00:01:00.594 --> 00:01:04.033
A strong acid ionises completely in water, which

00:01:04.114 --> 00:01:06.753
means every molecule gives its proton away and

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:08.113
forms ions.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:11.193
A weak acid ionises only partly.

00:01:11.474 --> 00:01:13.073
The pH meter sees this.

00:01:13.474 --> 00:01:16.753
A lower pH means more hydronium ions, and

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.673
each step of one on the pH scale

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.273
is ten times as many.

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:23.153
Two steps would be ten times ten, a

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:24.113
hundred times.

00:01:24.594 --> 00:01:27.233
The gap here is almost two steps, so

00:01:27.274 --> 00:01:29.793
the hydrochloric acid has about eighty times as

00:01:29.874 --> 00:01:31.393
many hydronium ions.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.000
Why does acetic acid stop at so few?

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.520
Its reaction runs both ways.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.080
Acetic acid molecules give protons to water, and

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.160
acetate ions take protons back from hydronium ions.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.720
Very soon both happen at the same rate,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.440
so you have a dynamic equilibrium, the balance

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.080
you met in Module 5, with most molecules

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.520
still whole.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.560
Chloride ions almost never take a proton back,

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.280
so hydrogen chloride goes all the way.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.960
A later video explains why.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.166
The equation has to say this.

00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:08.686
For a strong acid, write a single arrow:

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.846
the reaction goes to completion.

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.726
For a weak acid, write the double arrow,

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:16.286
because the forward and reverse reactions are both

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.246
going on.

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:20.206
Writing a double arrow for hydrochloric acid would

00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:23.486
say some molecules stay whole, and that's wrong.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.460
So which acids are strong?

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.060
For this course, learn six.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.740
A handy line is So I Brought No

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.460
Clean Clothes, and each word gives the key

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.900
letters of one acid, in order: sulfuric, for

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.100
its first proton, then hydroiodic, hydrobromic, nitric,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.580
hydrochloric and perchloric.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.380
Treat other acids as weak unless a question

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.500
tells you otherwise.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.420
And watch hydrofluoric acid: it looks like hydrochloric

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.940
acid, but it's weak.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:57.526
Now the second idea.

00:02:58.007 --> 00:03:00.726
Concentration is how much acid is dissolved in

00:03:00.887 --> 00:03:03.366
each litre, in moles per litre.

00:03:03.847 --> 00:03:06.046
It's a separate dial from strength.

00:03:06.407 --> 00:03:08.966
Add water to hydrochloric acid, and it becomes

00:03:09.007 --> 00:03:13.126
dilute, but every molecule still ionises, so it's

00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:16.286
a dilute strong acid, never a weak one.

00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:19.206
Dilute acetic acid, and a bigger fraction of

00:03:19.287 --> 00:03:22.326
its molecules ionise, but nowhere near all of

00:03:22.407 --> 00:03:24.646
them, so it's still a weak acid.

00:03:25.287 --> 00:03:29.206
That gives four combinations: concentrated or dilute, and

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:30.566
strong or weak.

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:32.966
Here's the surprise.

00:03:33.447 --> 00:03:35.713
One mole per litre acetic acid has a

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:38.166
pH of about two point four.

00:03:38.287 --> 00:03:40.966
Zero point zero zero one zero mole per

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:43.846
litre hydrochloric acid has a pH of three

00:03:44.007 --> 00:03:45.046
point zero.

00:03:45.087 --> 00:03:47.446
The weak acid has the lower pH, because

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:50.886
a thousand times more acid, even mostly whole,

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:53.446
still makes about four times as many hydronium

00:03:53.527 --> 00:03:54.486
ions.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.526
So pH alone can't tell you strength.

00:03:57.607 --> 00:04:00.326
To compare strength fairly, test acids at the

00:04:00.407 --> 00:04:02.006
same concentration.

00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:05.686
And weak doesn't mean safe: concentrated acetic acid

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.046
still burns skin.

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:10.026
Bases work the same way.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:13.226
A strong base such as sodium hydroxide is

00:04:13.307 --> 00:04:14.506
an ionic solid.

00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:18.586
When it dissolves, its ions separate completely, so

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:21.306
every formula unit gives a hydroxide ion.

00:04:21.787 --> 00:04:23.706
That's called dissociation.

00:04:24.027 --> 00:04:26.586
Ammonia is a weak base: as you saw

00:04:26.627 --> 00:04:29.466
in the models video, only some ammonia molecules

00:04:29.547 --> 00:04:30.986
take a proton from water.

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:34.186
At zero point one zero moles per litre,

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:38.266
sodium hydroxide reads pH thirteen, and ammonia only

00:04:38.387 --> 00:04:39.146
about eleven.

00:04:39.787 --> 00:04:43.626
Calcium, strontium and barium hydroxide are strong too,

00:04:43.707 --> 00:04:46.826
and each formula unit gives two hydroxide ions.

00:04:48.034 --> 00:04:52.033
Limewater is a saturated calcium hydroxide solution, which

00:04:52.114 --> 00:04:54.433
means it holds as much as can dissolve.

00:04:54.914 --> 00:04:56.833
It has a lower pH than that sodium

00:04:56.914 --> 00:04:58.073
hydroxide.

00:04:58.514 --> 00:05:00.753
So is calcium hydroxide weak?

00:05:01.314 --> 00:05:01.713
No.

00:05:02.274 --> 00:05:04.113
Less than two grams of it dissolves in

00:05:04.154 --> 00:05:05.953
a litre, and the rest sits at the

00:05:06.114 --> 00:05:06.353
bottom.

00:05:06.914 --> 00:05:09.873
But every bit that does dissolve splits completely

00:05:09.954 --> 00:05:10.833
into ions.

00:05:11.314 --> 00:05:13.793
That's low solubility, not weakness.

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:16.913
How much dissolves and how completely it splits

00:05:16.994 --> 00:05:18.753
up are different questions.

00:05:19.967 --> 00:05:21.326
So here's what to remember.

00:05:21.807 --> 00:05:24.046
Strength is how much it breaks up.

00:05:24.287 --> 00:05:27.006
Concentration is how much you put in.

00:05:27.087 --> 00:05:30.046
They're two separate dials, and the pH depends

00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:31.166
on both.

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:34.126
When you compare strength, keep the concentration the

00:05:34.327 --> 00:05:34.686
same.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:36.540
Your turn.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.460
Solutions of nitric acid and nitrous acid each

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.260
have a concentration of zero point one zero

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:44.540
moles per litre.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.980
Their pH values are one point zero and

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.220
two point two.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.140
Explain the difference in pH, and write an

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.580
ionic equation for each acid reacting with water,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:56.860
using the correct arrow.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.860
Pause here to answer.

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:12.306
Both have the same concentration, so the difference

00:06:12.387 --> 00:06:13.106
is strength.

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:17.426
Nitric acid is strong: it ionises completely, so

00:06:17.507 --> 00:06:18.786
it gets a single arrow.

00:06:19.347 --> 00:06:22.546
Nitrous acid is weak: only some molecules give

00:06:22.587 --> 00:06:24.786
a proton to water, so there are fewer

00:06:24.907 --> 00:06:27.666
hydronium ions and a higher pH, and it

00:06:27.747 --> 00:06:29.106
gets the double arrow.

00:06:29.427 --> 00:06:31.586
If you said nitrous acid must be more

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.506
dilute, look again: the concentrations are equal.

00:06:36.734 --> 00:06:37.373
Last one.

00:06:37.654 --> 00:06:40.573
A zero point one zero mole per litre

00:06:40.654 --> 00:06:43.853
ammonia solution has a pH of eleven point

00:06:43.934 --> 00:06:44.613
one.

00:06:44.974 --> 00:06:47.853
A zero point zero zero one zero mole

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:51.213
per litre sodium hydroxide solution has a pH

00:06:51.294 --> 00:06:53.013
of eleven point zero.

00:06:53.454 --> 00:06:55.853
A student claims the two bases are equally

00:06:56.014 --> 00:06:58.733
strong, because their pH values are almost the

00:06:58.814 --> 00:06:59.493
same.

00:06:59.774 --> 00:07:01.133
Assess this claim.

00:07:01.454 --> 00:07:02.733
Pause here to answer.

00:07:13.134 --> 00:07:15.013
The claim is not valid.

00:07:15.294 --> 00:07:18.893
Sodium hydroxide is strong, so every formula unit

00:07:18.974 --> 00:07:20.413
gives a hydroxide ion.

00:07:20.894 --> 00:07:23.773
Ammonia is weak, so only a small fraction

00:07:23.814 --> 00:07:25.893
of its molecules make hydroxide.

00:07:26.174 --> 00:07:28.893
The pH values match only because the ammonia

00:07:28.974 --> 00:07:31.213
is a hundred times more concentrated.

00:07:31.694 --> 00:07:34.973
At equal concentrations, sodium hydroxide would give the

00:07:35.134 --> 00:07:36.413
much higher pH.

00:07:36.974 --> 00:07:40.573
The classic slip is "same pH, same strength".

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.920
So whenever you compare acids or bases, ask

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.720
two separate questions.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.960
How much is dissolved per litre?

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.280
That's concentration.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.920
How completely does it ionise?

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:53.960
That's strength.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.360
Then use the arrow that matches.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.720
Next: measuring the pH of a range of

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.560
acids and bases in the lab.

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:04.206
Here are the key notes.

00:08:04.447 --> 00:08:06.366
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:06.447 --> 00:08:07.246
to copy them.
